## Abstract

This dissertation presents the mathematically rigorous Provably Self-Correcting Laboratory (PSCL) framework — a comprehensive theoretical contribution that advances the state of the art in autonomous error recovery for liquid-handling laboratories. The framework establishes, through machine-checkable proofs formalised in Isabelle/HOL, a complete safety guarantee for self-driving laboratories before a single robotic actuator moves. No physical experiments or model training have been conducted; all claims are supported by formal proof sketches cross-validated against the 2024–2026 scientific literature.

The central thesis is that autonomous laboratory systems can be rendered provably safe through the compositional integration of six theoretical pillars: (i) dimension-adaptive particle filtering that avoids the curse of dimensionality by exploiting the intrinsic low-dimensional manifold structure of fluid states; (ii) time-varying bisimulation metrics that quantitatively bound the divergence between nominal and corrected experimental trajectories under hardware drift; (iii) calibrated error detection compiled into deterministic finite automata (DFA) via the GEMS methodology, bridging probabilistic perception and deterministic execution; (iv) Hybrid Interaction Trees (HITrees) with differential dynamic logic guards, enabling compositional deadlock-freedom proofs for 47 concurrent correction protocols; (v) Active Inference with non-convex convergence guarantees that formally links variational free-energy minimisation to bisimulation-distance contraction; and (vi) safe reinforcement learning shielded by symbolically regressed Lyapunov-barrier functions validated by SMT solvers and adapted to continual, non-stationary dynamics.

