## 1. Introduction

### 1.1 The Challenge of Trustworthy Autonomy in Laboratory Science

Self-driving laboratories (SDLs) represent a paradigm shift in experimental science, combining robotics, artificial intelligence, and data science to automate the full Design-Make-Test-Analyse (DMTA) cycle. Recent market analyses project the global laboratory-automation market to reach approximately US $18.39 billion by 2033, growing at a compound annual rate of 9.3%. Nature named SDLs one of 2025's technologies to watch, and major investments — including Periodic Labs' $300 million seed round and Lila Sciences' $235 million funding — underscore the commercial momentum.

Yet safety remains the critical bottleneck. A Perspective published in Nature Chemistry Reviews in 2025 explicitly examines the development trajectory of SDLs, juxtaposing their evolution with other autonomous technologies and identifying safety as the central concern requiring immediate attention. The Royal Society published a comprehensive review in July 2025 examining both the technological capabilities and the policy implications of SDL deployment, noting that safety and security concerns are real but surmountable with proactive governance and human accountability. The inherent non-determinism, data-dependence, and lack of formal guarantees in AI-driven systems introduce fundamental challenges for verification, validation, and certification that traditional testing methodologies cannot address.

The Safe-SDL framework proposed in early 2026 further identifies a "Syntax-to-Safety Gap" — the disconnect between AI-generated syntactically correct commands and their physical safety implications — as the central unsolved challenge in SDL deployment. Formal verification techniques, including Control Barrier Functions and safety kernels, are identified as essential rather than optional components for any trustworthy SDL.

This dissertation addresses this challenge directly: it establishes that autonomous laboratories can be proved safe before a single robot moves — a paradigm shift from testing safety post hoc to constructing safety by mathematical proof.

### 1.2 The Provably Self-Correcting Laboratory Concept

The Provably Self-Correcting Laboratory (PSCL) is a theoretical architecture in which every component — from low-level state estimation to high-level error recovery — is subject to machine-checkable formal verification. The core insight is that safety is not an emergent property to be discovered by testing, but a mathematical invariant to be proved compositionally.

The framework comprises six integrated theoretical pillars:

**Pillar I — Dimension-Adaptive State Estimation.** A particle filter that exploits the intrinsic low-dimensional manifold structure of fluid states, achieving convergence rates independent of the ambient discretisation dimension. The manifold particle filter for state estimation on high-dimensional implicit manifolds demonstrates that the Riemannian manifold formulation of the state-space model avoids the curse of dimensionality from which most particle filter methods suffer, showing faster convergence and improved accuracy compared to conventional particle filters during persistent contact scenarios.

**Pillar II — Time-Varying Bisimulation Metrics.** Quantitative refinement bounds between nominal and corrected experimental trajectories that remain valid under gradual hardware drift. Bisimulation metrics for Markov decision processes, defined as fixed points of operators based on the Wasserstein distance between transition distributions, provide a principled measure of behavioural similarity between states. This dissertation extends these metrics to time-varying transition kernels.

**Pillar III — Calibrated-to-Deterministic Compilation.** A GEMS-style transformation of probabilistic error detectors into deterministic finite automata, providing a guaranteed decision boundary that bridges probabilistic perception and deterministic execution.

**Pillar IV — Hybrid Interaction Trees.** An extension of the ITree formalism with differential dynamic logic guards, enabling compositional deadlock-freedom proofs for concurrent correction protocols. Foster et al.'s mechanisation of Interaction Trees in Isabelle/HOL, producing an execution and verification framework for coinductive structures that encode infinite labelled transition systems yet remain inherently executable, provides the essential foundation. KeYmaera X, an axiomatic tactical theorem prover for hybrid systems with a minimal soundness-critical core of approximately 2,000 lines of code, provides the dL verification backend.

**Pillar V — Active Inference with Formal Safety Links.** Non-convex convergence guarantees for belief updating, with a provable connection between variational free-energy minimisation and bisimulation-distance reduction. Active inference, grounded in the Free Energy Principle, provides a unifying framework combining utility with epistemic drives for uncertainty resolution.

**Pillar VI — Symbolic Lyapunov Shielding.** A transformer-based symbolic regression pipeline for synthesising Lyapunov-barrier functions in belief space, validated by SMT solvers and adapted to continual, non-stationary settings. The SR4BC tool, implementing a learner-distiller-verifier loop where neural barrier candidates are distilled via symbolic regression and verified by an SMT solver, and the CoNSAL framework, constructing analytical Lyapunov functions by distilling neural network models into precise analytical forms, provide the direct antecedents.

