## 2. Literature Review

### 2.1 Self-Driving Laboratories: State of the Art

The Royal Society published in July 2025 a comprehensive review of SDLs examining both technological capabilities and policy implications, noting that today's most capable SDLs automate nearly the entire scientific method, from hypothesis generation and experimental design through execution, data analysis, and hypothesis updating. Applications span organic synthesis, materials science, and biological sciences, with cloud laboratories increasingly enabling SDL-as-a-service paradigms.

The 2025 Nature Chemistry Reviews Perspective on steering towards safe SDLs identifies robust error recovery, safety guarantees under hardware degradation, and formal verification of autonomous decision-making as critical open problems. The Safe-SDL framework of 2026 identifies the Syntax-to-Safety Gap as the central challenge, arguing that architectural safety mechanisms built on formally verified components are essential rather than optional.

### 2.2 Formal Verification for Autonomous Systems

A 2026 survey from Purdue University provides a comprehensive overview of formal methods in robot policy learning and verification. A 2025 structured literature review identifies increasing use of formal synthesis and probabilistic verification in robotic autonomous systems.

Conformal prediction has emerged as a statistical tool for uncertainty quantification in learning-enabled autonomous systems. Control Barrier Functions (CBFs), with extensions to learning-based and multi-agent settings, provide provable safety for continuous dynamics. Formal verification through KeYmaera X, an axiomatic tactical theorem prover for hybrid systems featuring a minimal core of just about 2,000 lines of code that isolates all soundness-critical reasoning, provides the definitive foundation for dL-based verification in this dissertation.

### 2.3 Particle Filtering on Manifolds

The curse of dimensionality in particle filtering has been extensively studied. Under fairly weak assumptions, if the ensemble size grows sub-exponentially in the cube root of the system dimension, the maximum of particle weights converges to unity, causing practical filter collapse. The Manifold Particle Filter (MPF) for state estimation on high-dimensional implicit manifolds demonstrates that the Riemannian manifold formulation of the state-space model avoids the curse of dimensionality, showing faster convergence and improved accuracy compared to conventional particle filters during persistent contact scenarios. Said and Manton provide a rigorous convergence proof for particle filtering with observations on a manifold, under the assumption of compactness.

### 2.4 Bisimulation Metrics for Stochastic and Hybrid Systems

Bisimulation metrics provide a quantitative generalisation of classical bisimulation relations for probabilistic systems. Bisimulation metrics for MDPs are defined by Ferns et al. as fixed points of operators based on the Wasserstein distance between transition distributions. Recent work at NeurIPS 2024 establishes that bisimulation metrics are in fact optimal transport distances and can be computed efficiently via Sinkhorn value iteration. Recent work further applies bisimulation metrics to model predictive control, demonstrating improved training stability, robustness against input noise, and computational efficiency. The extension to time-varying transition kernels — essential for systems subject to hardware drift — is a novel theoretical contribution of this dissertation.

### 2.5 Interaction Trees in Isabelle/HOL

Foster et al. mechanised Interaction Trees in Isabelle/HOL to produce an execution and verification framework unifying model execution and deductive verification. ITrees are coinductive structures that allow encoding of infinite labelled transition systems while remaining inherently executable. The framework has been extended to verified numerical methods, enabling declaration of numerical programs annotated with variants and invariants for reasoning about correctness specifications. Further developments include formally verified simulations of state-rich processes using Interaction Trees, with applications to CSP and Circus languages formally linking the new semantics with the failures-divergences semantic model. The extension of ITrees to hybrid systems — combining continuous physical dynamics with discrete control transitions — is a novel contribution of this dissertation.

### 2.6 Active Inference and the Free Energy Principle

Active Inference (AIF) unifies perception, action, and learning under the imperative of minimising variational free energy. A central challenge in artificial intelligence and cognitive science is identifying a unifying principle that governs inference, learning, and action; active inference proposes such a principle — the minimisation of variational free energy — showing formal equivalences with Bayesian decision theory, resource rationality, optimal control, and reinforcement learning. De Vries et al. (2025) show that introducing an epistemic prior into the generative model redefines expected free energy minimisation as a standard variational free energy problem, establishing that AIF is properly a subtype of variational inference. A novel message-passing scheme for factored-state MDPs enables scalable active inference, overcoming the intractability of high-dimensional planning.

### 2.7 Symbolic Regression for Lyapunov and Barrier Functions

The SR4BC tool implements a learner-distiller-verifier loop where neural barrier candidates are distilled via symbolic regression and then verified by an SMT solver, with counterexamples fed back to guide further learning, demonstrating that this approach is much more efficient and effective than state-of-the-art approaches. The CoNSAL framework constructs analytical Lyapunov functions for nonlinear dynamical systems by distilling neural network models into precise analytical forms, applying symbolic regression not only as a tool for translation but also as a means to uncover counterexamples, terminating when no counterexamples are found in the analytical formulation. An RL-based generative approach uses a symbolic transformer model to generate Lyapunov functions in clear analytical form, verifying Lyapunov conditions via localised sampling in neighbourhoods of minimisers and incorporating identified counterexamples into the training set for further optimisation.

### 2.8 Byzantine-Robust Federated Learning

Federated learning enables collaborative model training across decentralised data sources without sharing raw data, but faces security bottlenecks from poisoning attacks. Cambus et al. (2025) investigate coordinate-wise median aggregation in both centralised and decentralised federated learning, proposing the Minimum Diameter and Hyperbox algorithms that prove both satisfy the box validity condition and tolerate Byzantine failures. The coordinate-wise median is scalable, resists outliers, and ensures robust aggregation, unlike the mean whose breakdown point is zero.

### 2.9 Differential Dynamic Logic and KeYmaera X

Differential dynamic logic (dL) provides a formal framework for specifying and verifying properties of hybrid systems. KeYmaera X is an axiomatic tactical theorem prover for hybrid systems featuring a minimal microkernel of approximately 2,000 lines of code that isolates all soundness-critical reasoning. KeYmaera X analyses the control program and the physical behaviour of the controlled system together in differential dynamic logic. Case studies include adaptive cruise control, autonomous automobile control, the European Train Control System, aircraft collision avoidance, autonomous robots, and surgical robots.

### 2.10 Categorical Unification through Distributive Laws

Distributive laws provide the categorical mechanism for composing monadic effects. The 2025 literature includes advances in parametric distributive laws for uniform monad composition, and monotone weak distributive laws over the lifted powerset monad in categories of algebras. The specific composition of the Interaction Tree monad with a POMDP belief-coalgebra functor through a distributive law has not been previously addressed in the literature, representing a novel categorical contribution of this dissertation.

### 2.11 Gaps Addressed by This Dissertation

The literature reveals seven critical gaps: (1) no unified verification framework integrates particle filtering, error detection, correction protocols, safe RL, and active inference under a single formal safety guarantee for laboratory automation; (2) manifold-adaptive convergence results with formal bounds for high-dimensional fluid state filtering have not been developed; (3) time-varying bisimulation metrics for hardware-drifting systems have not been formalised; (4) hybrid extensions of Interaction Trees with dL guards and compositional deadlock-freedom proofs are absent; (5) the formal connection between variational free-energy minimisation and bisimulation-distance reduction has not been established; (6) symbolic regression for Lyapunov shielding in belief space with SMT validation and continual adaptation represents an unexplored combination; (7) categorical unification of ITrees, POMDPs, and Active Inference through a distributive law has not been attempted.

