## 7. Conclusion and Future Work

### 7.1 Summary of Contributions

This dissertation, authored by Ouadi Maakoul, has presented a comprehensive theoretical framework for the Provably Self-Correcting Laboratory. The eleven principal contributions are:

**Contribution 1 — Dimension-Adaptive Particle Filtering.** A formal convergence result proving that particle filters for high-dimensional fluid states achieve the optimal $d$-dimensional rate when the state distribution concentrates on an intrinsic manifold. Proof avoids the curse of dimensionality by performing resampling in the estimated tangent space, building on and extending the Manifold Particle Filter literature.

**Contribution 2 — Time-Varying Bisimulation Metrics.** The first extension of bisimulation metrics to time-varying transition kernels, with existence and Lipschitz continuity proved under slow variation, enabling quantitative refinement bounds that remain valid under hardware drift. This extends the bisimulation-as-optimal-transport framework established in the 2024 NeurIPS literature.

**Contribution 3 — GEMS-Style Deterministic Compilation.** A formal guarantee that the calibrated probabilistic error detector can be compiled into a DFA while preserving the MAP classification with arbitrarily high probability, bridging the probabilistic perception and deterministic execution layers.

**Contribution 4 — Hybrid Interaction Trees.** The first extension of ITrees with differential dynamic logic guards for continuous evolution and conditional jumps, combined with a compositional deadlock-freedom proof for resource-disjoint concurrent HITree instances. This extends Foster et al.'s Isabelle/HOL ITree framework to the hybrid systems domain, integrating KeYmaera X's dL verification.

**Contribution 5 — Active Inference–Bisimulation Link.** A formal connection between variational free-energy minimisation and bisimulation-distance reduction: minimising expected free energy directly tightens the refinement bound. Non-convex convergence guarantees are established under the Polyak–Łojasiewicz condition, extending the active-inference-as-variational-inference connection established in the 2025 literature.

**Contribution 6 — Symbolic Lyapunov Shielding.** A complete pipeline for synthesising Lyapunov-barrier functions in belief space using transformer-based symbolic regression with SMT validation, with continual adaptation to non-stationary dynamics. This unifies and extends the SR4BC and CoNSAL approaches to the belief-space setting.

**Contribution 7 — Federated Safe Learning.** Median-based robust aggregation with certified breakdown point and Lyapunov witness certificates, ensuring the aggregated policy preserves safety under Byzantine attacks. This builds on and extends the Cambus et al. (2025) coordinate-wise median framework.

**Contribution 8 — dL-Verified Digital Twin.** Formal verification of the digital twin's safety properties in KeYmaera X, with tighter sim-to-real transfer bounds using Lipschitz dynamics.

**Contribution 9 — Coalgebraic Novelty Expansion.** A refinement-based approach to verified recipe book expansion, proving that adding new correction protocols monotonically improves the LLR.

**Contribution 10 — Global Rely-Guarantee and Quantitative LLR.** A compositional safety proof establishing a global safety invariant, with a computable quantitative metric bounding the cumulative probability of catastrophic failure.

**Contribution 11 — Categorical Unification.** An explicit distributive law between the ITree monad and the POMDP belief-coalgebra functor, yielding a combined monad in which the global safety invariant corresponds to a bisimulation — a novel categorical construction not previously addressed in the literature.

### 7.2 Limitations

This is a purely theoretical contribution. The following limitations are acknowledged:

**Incomplete Formalisation.** The Isabelle/HOL formalisation is in progress. Only the 10 core HITree instances have been fully machine-checked. The remaining 37 protocols, the full distributive law, and the LLR sensitivity analysis require additional effort.

**Idealised Assumptions.** Several theorems rely on assumptions (smoothness, compactness, reach, PL condition) that may not hold exactly in real laboratory environments. The framework provides a theoretical upper bound on safety; practical deployment requires empirical validation of these assumptions.

**Computational Feasibility.** The symbolic regression pipeline, particle filter with manifold projection, and real-time SMT solving impose computational demands that may exceed current edge hardware capabilities.

**No Physical Validation.** No experiments have been conducted. The theorems guarantee safety under the stated assumptions; whether these assumptions are satisfied by real liquid handlers remains to be empirically determined.

### 7.3 Roadmap for Future Work

**Phase 1 (Months 1–11) — Complete the Isabelle/HOL formalisation:** Finish machine-checking the remaining 37 HITree instances (6 person-months); formalise the full distributive law and verify the four axioms (3 person-months); implement the LLR metric computation and verify the monotonicity property (2 person-months).

**Phase 2 (Months 12–13) — Sensitivity analysis:** Perform rigorous sensitivity analysis of the LLR metric under varying drift assumptions.

**Phase 3 (Months 14–16) — Scalability analysis:** Analyse computational complexity of the symbolic regression pipeline as a function of belief-space dimension $m$; develop approximation schemes with formal error bounds.

**Phase 4 (Months 17–20) — Relaxation of assumptions:** Investigate generalisation under weaker assumptions (non-compact manifolds, non-smooth dynamics) using geometric measure theory.

**Phase 5 (Months 21–23) — Integration with laboratory standards:** Map the framework's safety integrity levels to IEC 61508 and ISO 13485; develop a formal argument for certification credit.

Only after this theoretical foundation is fully solidified — with all proofs machine-checked and all assumptions explicitly verified — do we advocate for experimental implementation.

### 7.4 Closing Remarks

This dissertation demonstrates that autonomous laboratories can be proved safe by mathematical construction. The framework provides a blueprint for trustworthy self-driving laboratories that can operate with formal guarantees — even under error conditions, hardware drift, and adversarial perturbations.

The paradigm shift established here — from testing safety to proving safety — has implications beyond laboratory automation, extending to any safety-critical autonomous system where formal verification can replace empirical validation. The Provably Self-Correcting Laboratory framework establishes that the question "Is this autonomous system safe?" can, in principle, be answered with mathematical certainty before the first robot moves.

